A quick edit to my message (I should have check for it before...):

I've been able to recover the same results with some change in the DSM function:

> mod2.alt.km.new2 <- dsm(Dhat~s(X,Y),ddf.obj = detect.hn3$ddf,segment.data = segdata,
+                         observation.data = obsdata2,segment.area = pointdata$area3,
+                         engine = "gam",method = "GCV.Cp")

And the comparison of results:
> summary(mod2.alt.km.new2)

Family: quasipoisson
Link function: log

Formula:
Dhat ~ s(X, Y)

Parametric coefficients:
            Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)
(Intercept)   2.2500     0.1519   14.82   <2e-16 ***
---
Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1

Approximate significance of smooth terms:
       edf Ref.df     F p-value
s(X,Y)   2      2 1.909   0.154

R-sq.(adj) =  0.0148   Deviance explained = 4.65%
GCV = 15.117  Scale est. = 17.901    n = 100

> summary(mod2.alt.km <http://mod2.alt.km>)

Family: quasipoisson
Link function: log

Formula:
Dhat ~ s(X, Y)

Parametric coefficients:
            Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)
(Intercept)   2.2500     0.1519   14.82   <2e-16 ***
---
Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1

Approximate significance of smooth terms:
       edf Ref.df     F p-value
s(X,Y)   2      2 1.909   0.154

R-sq.(adj) =  0.0148   Deviance explained = 4.65%
GCV = 15.117  Scale est. = 17.901    n = 100

So, the most important change appears to be that default estimation method for smoothing is now REML insteand of GCV. So, my question now is: what is the best option?
Are the results with GCV less robust than with REML ?

Cheers,

Guillaume

