>
> So, the most important change appears to be that default estimation
> method for smoothing is now REML insteand of GCV. So, my question now
> is: what is the best option?
> Are the results with GCV less robust than with REML ?
>
>
> Cheers,
>
> Guillaume
>
> On Friday, 31 March 2017 14:37:23 UTC+2, Guillaume Souchay wrote:
>
>     Dear list-folks and DS team,
>
>     I'm currently trying to produce a small report on DSM and its use
>     with point-transect sampling (as I promised several months ago to
>     Eric and I think it could be quite useful for sharing), as I did
>     this during the 2016 workshop in St Andrews with the help of CREEM crew.
>
>     I ran the analysis in last August and here is the summary of the dsm
>     function:
>     |

>     >summary(mod2.alt.km <http://mod2.alt.km> <http://mod2.alt.km>)

>
>     Family: quasipoisson
>     Link function: log
>
>     Formula:
>     Dhat ~ s(X, Y)
>
>     Parametric coefficients:
>                 Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)
>     (Intercept)   2.2500     0.1519   14.82   <2e-16 ***
>     ---
>     Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1
>
>     Approximate significance of smooth terms:
>            edf Ref.df     F p-value
>     s(X,Y)   2      2 1.909   0.154
>
>     R-sq.(adj) =  0.0148   Deviance explained = 4.65%
>     GCV = 15.117  Scale est. = 17.901    n = 100
>     |
>
>     When I was trying to redo the analysis (I'm creating a knitr PDF
>     with RStudio), the results were quite different, so I decided to run
>     the exact same model and here is the summary:
>     |
>     > summary(mod2.alt.km.new <http://mod2.alt.km.new>)
>
>     Family: quasipoisson
>     Link function: log
>
>     Formula:
>     Dhat ~ s(X, Y)
>
>     Parametric coefficients:
>                 Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)
>     (Intercept)   2.1750     0.1438   15.12   <2e-16 ***
>     ---
>     Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1
>
>     Approximate significance of smooth terms:
>              edf Ref.df    F p-value
>     s(X,Y) 11.88  16.18 1.31   0.208
>
>     R-sq.(adj) =   0.21   Deviance explained = 29.3%
>     -REML = 191.99  Scale est. = 14.545    n = 100
>     |
>
>     It appears that the estimation method has changed (REML instead of
>     CGV.Cp) and the deviance explained is now much bigger, despite the
>     dataset doesn't change.
>     The parametric coefficient is lower now and the array of estimated
>     degrees of freedom for the model terms was only 2 and it now close
>     to 12 ...
>
>     Furthermore, the predicted values are now different.
>     Here is the range of the predicted values with the "old" dsm:
>     |
>     > mod2.alt.km.pred <- predict(mod2.alt.km <http://mod2.alt.km>

>     <http://mod2.alt.km>,preddata,preddata$area2)

>     > range(mod2.alt.km.pred)
>     [1]  5.263684 19.122528
>     > summary(mod2.alt.km.pred)
>        Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.
>       5.264   7.716   9.589  10.130  11.920  19.120
>     |
>     Densities are now estimated between 5 and 20 ind/km2.
>
>     Here is the range of the predicted values with the "new" dsm with
>     exactly the same data for prediction:
>     |
>     > mod2.alt.km.pred.new <http://mod2.alt.km.pred.new> <-
>     predict(mod2.alt.km.new <http://mod2.alt.km.new>,preddata,preddata$area2)  #relationship
>     between Dhat and covariates
>     > range(mod2.alt.km.pred.new <http://mod2.alt.km.pred.new>)
>     [1]  3.715586 52.015128
>     > summary(mod2.alt.km.pred.new <http://mod2.alt.km.pred.new>)
>        Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.
>       3.716   6.198   8.917  10.430  11.540  52.020
>     |
>
>     Well, the lower density is now 3.7, not so different, but the larger
>     predicted value is higher than 50 ind/km2, so about 2.5 times higher
>     and it seems less biologically relevant ...
>     Despite the mean predicted densities are not so different, it seems
>     that it works quite differently for both tails of the distribution
>     of predicted values.
>
>     It is quite a big issue because when mapping such results, it can be
>     quite difficult to explain why the scale of density is so big for
>     some points.
>
>     Do you have any idea of what happened between August 2016 and now?
>     The dsm package has been updated maybe and some function may have
>     change ...
>
>     Thanks a lot for any clue !
>
>     Guillaume
